#  Constructors in Java

##  What is a Constructor


A constructor is a special method used to initialize objects.



## Key Features

* Same name as the class
* No return type (not even `void`)
* Called automatically when object is created
* Used to initialize object data



## Syntax

class ClassName {

    ClassName() {
        // constructor body
    }
}




## Example

class Student {

    String name;

    Student() {
        name = "Default Name";
    }
}




## Types of Constructors

### 1. Default Constructor

A constructor with no parameters

### Example

class Car {

    Car() {
        System.out.println("Car created");
    }
}


---

###  2. Parameterized Constructor

A constructor with parameters to initialize values

### Example
class Car {

    String model;
    int speed;

    Car(String model, int speed) {
        this.model = model;
        this.speed = speed;
    }
}




###  3. Copy Constructor (Conceptual)

Creates a new object using another object's values

### Example

class Car {

    String model;

    Car(Car c) {
        this.model = c.model;
    }
}


## this Keyword in Constructor

Refers to the current object


### Example

class Student {

    String name;

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}


## Constructor Overloading


Multiple constructors with different parameters

### Example

class Car {

    String model;
    int speed;

    Car() {
        model = "Default";
    }

    Car(String model) {
        this.model = model;
    }

    Car(String model, int speed) {
        this.model = model;
        this.speed = speed;
    }
}




## Constructor Chaining


Calling one constructor from another using `this()`


###  Example

class Car {

    String model;
    int speed;

    Car() {
        this("Default", 0);
    }

    Car(String model, int speed) {
        this.model = model;
        this.speed = speed;
    }
}




##  super() in Constructors

Used to call parent class constructor


### Example

class Animal {
    Animal() {
        System.out.println("Animal created");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    Dog() {
        super();
        System.out.println("Dog created");
    }
}




## Important Rules

* Constructor name must match class name
* No return type
* Called automatically when object is created
* If no constructor is written → Java provides default constructor



## Difference: Constructor vs Method

| Feature     | Constructor          | Method            |
| ----------- | -------------------- | ----------------- |
| Name        | Same as class        | Any name          |
| Return type | No return type       | Has return type   |
| Purpose     | Initialize object    | Perform operation |
| Invocation  | Called automatically | Called manually   |



##  Best Practices

* Use constructors to initialize required fields
* Prefer parameterized constructors
* Use `this` for clarity
* Avoid too many constructors (use builder/setters if needed)
